// The DRIVERS' report path — the erased sink, kept OUT of diagnostics.npk.
//
// Why its own module: `REACH` is IMPORT-scoped, so an async function anywhere in
// a module makes `DeadlineExceeded` reachable in every program that imports it —
// an async body can be CANCELLED, which is also why TYPE-037 refuses
// `async` + `never fails`. Putting these three functions in diagnostics.npk made
// twelve of the compiler's OWN unit tests grow a `(DeadlineExceeded)` arm,
// including `tests/backend/ir_expr.npk`, which does not mention the report path
// at all. An arm acknowledging something that cannot happen in that program is
// an acknowledgement worth less than none — the exhaustive `failsafe` (D-179)
// earns its keep by being TRUE, so the fix is to put the async surface where it
// belongs rather than to spend the rule's credibility.
//
// diagnostics.npk keeps what it always was: the list, the diagnostic, and
// `diag_line`, the ONE formatter (1.0.8). This file is the one WRITER --
// hence `diag_writer`, since a module may not share a name with a function
// it declares and `diag_report` is the walk's own name.

mod:diag_writer;

use "./diagnostics.npk".*;
use "./source_location.npk".*;
use "./source.npk".*;


// HOW LONG A DIAGNOSTIC WRITE MAY TAKE. The std descriptors stay BLOCKING --
// the one stated D-071 exception (1.1.12c) -- so this bounds a pathological
// pipe reader rather than an ordinary write, and it is generous on purpose: a
// report that gives up is a defect the reader never learns about.
fixed Duration:DIAG_WITHIN = Duration{ ns: 5000000000i64 };

// THE ONE ERASED DIAGNOSTIC WALK. Five copies of this loop existed -- `report`
// in src/main.npk, `report` in tools/check.npk, and three inline walks across
// resolve_check and parse_check -- and the 1.0.8 note further up this file
// records exactly what letting a second copy exist costs. The sink is
// `dyn Writer`, so WHICH writer decides where the text lands: the drivers pass
// stderr, and a capture buffer would be the same call.
//
// `dyn Writer` passes BY VALUE, never through a pointer: a trait method's
// receiver is `Self->`, so the erased call takes the sink's own address
// (1.1.12d), and `dyn Writer->:sink` fails method lookup outright.
//
// BEST-EFFORT BY THE TIME IT PRINTS, deliberately (D-163's reasoned `?|`): a
// broken stderr cannot be reported on stderr, so each write swallows its own
// Result rather than inventing a failure nobody can act on.
// NOT `never fails`, and it cannot be: TYPE-037 refuses the pair, because a
// suspended task can be CANCELLED and cancellation is an error the contract
// would be denying (D-163). The best-effort promise this walk actually keeps is
// per-write -- each `?|` below -- rather than a signature-level one.
pub async func:diag_report = NIL(dyn Writer:sink, DiagList->:list,
                                 SourceManager->:sm, Duration:within) {
    int64:n = raw diaglist_count(list);
    int64:d = 0i64;
    while (d < n) {
        string:ln = string_concat(raw diag_line(raw diaglist_at(list, d), sm),
                                  "\n");
        discard(await sink.write(string_bytes(ln), within) ?| 0i64);
        d = d + 1i64;
    }
    if (list.overflowed) {
        string:ov = string_concat(raw diag_overflow_line(), "\n");
        discard(await sink.write(string_bytes(ov), within) ?| 0i64);
    }
    await sink.flush(within) ?| NIL;
    pass NIL;
};

// The drivers' one-line spelling. A `dyn` is MOVE-ONLY, so a by-value sink
// cannot be reused across calls and there is no "construct stderr once" to be
// had -- every reporting site is its own early exit, so each builds its own,
// and the dup it owns is closed by the OwnedFd drop at this function's exit.
pub async func:diag_report_stderr = NIL(DiagList->:list, SourceManager->:sm) {
    TextWriter<ByteWriter>:tw = relay std_err();
    dyn Writer:sink = move(tw);
    await diag_report(sink, list, sm, DIAG_WITHIN) ?| NIL;
    pass NIL;
};

// A driver's own message -- "cannot write the output file" -- through the SAME
// sink as the diagnostics. Two paths to one stream is the thing the blueprint
// philosophy rejects, and it is what this file's own 1.0.8 note is about.
pub async func:diag_say = NIL(string:msg) {
    TextWriter<ByteWriter>:tw = relay std_err();
    dyn Writer:sink = move(tw);
    string:ln = string_concat(msg, "\n");
    discard(await sink.write(string_bytes(ln), DIAG_WITHIN) ?| 0i64);
    await sink.flush(DIAG_WITHIN) ?| NIL;
    pass NIL;
};
